  "description": "Aspose.Page.Drawing is the cross-platform build of Aspose.Page for .NET — it converts PostScript (PS), EPS and XPS documents to PDF and to raster images on Linux, macOS and in containers, with no native dependencies.\n\nUse this package on Linux, macOS, Docker, Kubernetes, AWS Lambda and Azure Functions. On Windows and .NET Framework, use Aspose.Page instead. That package depends on System.Drawing.Common, which has been Windows-only since .NET 7 — this build replaces it with Aspose.Drawing, a fully managed graphics library. Install one package or the other, not both.\n\nThe API is identical to Aspose.Page. If you convert or read documents, no code changes are needed when switching. Only code that constructs PS or XPS documents needs its System.Drawing using-aliases repointed at Aspose.Page.Drawing; the type names are unchanged.\n\nThe API helps developers create and edit XPS documents while working with canvas, paths and glyph elements. Utilize a set of primitives such as elliptical arcs, straight lines and Bezier curve segments to create vector graphics. Add default print tickets and preserve them in the XPS file, link print ticket objects among job, document and page, and work with Print Schema model classes for ticket manipulation. Gradients, visual brushes and transparency are supported.\n\nAspose.Page.Drawing supports the PostScript language levels 1-3, with the exception of some font types. It saves PS and EPS files to PDF and converts PS, EPS and XPS documents to BMP, TIFF, JPEG and PNG, as well as to EMF, WMF and GIF.\n\nWritten entirely in C# and distributed as a single managed assembly with no native binaries. Targets .NET Standard 2.0 and later and .NET 7.0 and later, so it also covers .NET Core 2.x and .NET 8 and later.",
